# The registered wired identity of this machine's Tencent USB Ethernet adapter.
#
# This is device identity, not network classification. Which Ethernet link is the office LAN is
# decided by 802.1X authorization in scripts/network-reconfigure, and must not be inferred from an
# adapter. This file does the unrelated job of presenting the hardware address Tencent's NAC has on
# file for this adapter, which its EAP-TLS authorization depends on.
#
# Matched on the adapter's permanent address rather than the USB port it happens to occupy. Until
# 2026-09-20 this matched Path=pci-0000:00:14.0-usb-0:1:1.0; the adapter moved to usb-0:6:1.0, the
# override silently stopped applying, and EAP-TLS failed from that point on. Both halves are in the
# journal: with the override applied the link came up as fe80::a3a:88ff:fe5a:b537 and authorized
# into 10.76.165.31/24 on 2026-08-24, and without it the link came up as fe80::20e:c6ff:fe5d:e788
# and was held at suppPortStatus=Unauthorized on the 10.76.76.0/26 quarantine lease on 2026-09-20.
# The permanent address survives moving the adapter, so this match cannot fail the same way.
#
# Deleting this file that day was the wrong reading of the same evidence: an override that is not
# applying is one to repair, not to remove.
#
# NamePolicy and AlternativeNamesPolicy are copied verbatim from 99-default.link. The first matching
# .link replaces that file outright for this device, and `keep` is what preserves the enp9s0u2u1u2
# name that udev/90-wired-8021x.rules and the wpa_supplicant@ instance are both written against.
[Match]
PermanentMACAddress=00:0e:c6:5d:e7:88
Type=ether

[Link]
MACAddress=08:3a:88:5a:b5:37
NamePolicy=keep kernel database onboard slot path
AlternativeNamesPolicy=database onboard slot path mac
